---
import { getEntry } from 'astro:content';
import Head from '../../components/Head.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import '../../styles/global.css';

const entry = await getEntry('entries', 'pacific-pulse');
if (!entry) throw new Error('Pacific Pulse content entry is missing.');
const { title, description, date } = entry.data;
const jsonLd = { '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'CreativeWork', headline: title, name: title, description, datePublished: date?.toISOString(), author: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Andrew James', url: 'https://andjam.es' } };
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <Head {title} {description} {jsonLd} />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="/static/kelvinwaves/demo/styles.css" />
    <style is:inline>nav.nav { height:auto; max-width:none; margin:0; padding:0; background:rgba(245,242,235,.78); border-bottom:1px solid rgba(217,213,204,.72); font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif; letter-spacing:normal; backdrop-filter:blur(14px); -webkit-backdrop-filter:blur(14px); } .nav .nav-inner { padding:.55rem 0; } .nav .brand { font-size:1rem; } .nav .nav-links { gap:1.25rem; } .nav .nav-links a { font-size:.8rem; } .nav .nav-links .btn { padding:.38rem .75rem; } #kelvin-demo > main { width:100%; max-width:none; min-height:0; margin:0; padding:0; }</style>
  </head>
  <body>
    <Nav />
    <div id="kelvin-demo" class="demo-page">
      <svg class="demo-water-fill" id="intro-water-fill" viewBox="0 0 1440 900" preserveAspectRatio="none" aria-hidden="true"><defs><linearGradient id="intro-water-gradient" x1="0" x2="0" y1="0" y2="1"><stop offset="0" stop-color="#267b8d" stop-opacity=".82"/><stop offset=".5" stop-color="#0c5163" stop-opacity=".94"/><stop offset="1" stop-color="#071c26"/></linearGradient></defs><path id="water-body"></path></svg>
    <main>
      <section class="demo-scene demo-scene--intro">
        <div class="demo-sticky demo-sticky--intro"><svg id="intro-wave-chart" class="demo-intro-waves" aria-label="Opening view of measured non-tidal residual traces"></svg><div class="demo-chart-shell demo-chart-shell--intro"><div class="demo-chart-kicker">Non-tidal water level changes, in centimeters</div><svg id="intro-chart" aria-label="Non-tidal sea-level residuals at California tide gauges"></svg></div></div>
        <article class="demo-story-step demo-step demo-story-step--hero" data-scene="hero"><div class="demo-story-card demo-story-card--hero"><p class="demo-eyebrow">An attempt at a live<i>(-ish)</i> coastal investigation</p><h1>Can we watch a Kelvin wave move up the California coast?</h1><p class="demo-deck">NOAA collects tide data along the California coastline. I wanted to see if tide gauges might show <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kelvin_wave" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Kelvin waves</a> moving along the coast.</p><p class="demo-scroll-hint">Scroll to investigate <span>↓</span></p></div></article>
        <article class="demo-story-step demo-step" data-scene="lines"><div class="demo-story-card"><p class="demo-card-number">Step 01</p><p>Kelvin waves aren't just another wave. They're disturbances that can travel along coastlines, affecting sea level and sometimes the structure of the water below.</p><p>Below, each line is an observed water level at a station, minus the tide prediction for that location.</p><p class="demo-muted">According to their Wiki, surface-level Kelvins can move very fast. The internal waves (bringing warm water) travel at a slower pace.</p></div></article>
      </section>
      <section class="demo-scene demo-scene--historic">
        <div class="demo-sticky"><div class="demo-chart-shell demo-chart-shell--historic"><div class="demo-chart-kicker">Historical benchmark · NOAA CO-OPS · 2015–16</div><h3>Before 2026: what did an El Niño winter look like at the gauges?</h3><svg id="historic-chart" aria-label="Historical tide-removed water-level residuals at four California tide gauges during the 2015–16 El Niño"></svg></div></div>
        <article class="demo-story-step demo-step" data-scene="historic"><div class="demo-story-card"><p class="demo-card-number">Historical benchmark</p><p>This uses NOAA’s verified hourly water levels from October 2015 through April 2016. Each line is observed water level minus its local predicted astronomical tide, shown as a 48-hour average.</p><p class="demo-muted">The 2015–16 El Niño is a documented period of unusual coastal conditions. This chart establishes context; it does not assign every rise in a gauge record to one Kelvin wave.</p></div></article>
        <article class="demo-story-step demo-step demo-story-step--detail" data-scene="historic" data-highlight="historic-context"><div class="demo-story-card demo-story-card--detail"><p class="demo-card-number">Historical context</p><p>Southern California water levels were documented as persistently elevated during this El Niño. The shaded November–December interval marks that reported high-water context—not a confirmed arrival time for a single wave.</p><p class="demo-muted">The test we carry forward: do short-term features show up later at stations farther north? The following 2026 charts use that same question.</p><p class="demo-source-line">Sources: <a href="https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/news/dec15/california-flooding.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">NOAA Ocean Service</a> · <a href="https://agupubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1029/2018JF004771" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">peer-reviewed coastal analysis</a></p></div></article>
      </section>
      <section class="demo-scene demo-scene--historic-sst">
        <div class="demo-sticky"><div class="demo-chart-shell demo-chart-shell--historic-sst"><div class="demo-chart-kicker">Historical temperature context · NOAA OISST · Baja to Oregon</div><h3>Was warmer-than-normal water already present farther south?</h3><svg id="historic-sst-chart" aria-label="Historical sea surface temperature anomalies from Baja California to Oregon"></svg></div></div>
        <article class="demo-story-step demo-step" data-scene="historic-sst"><div class="demo-story-card"><p class="demo-card-number">A wider view</p><p>Daily offshore sea-surface temperature anomalies run from Baja California to Oregon. Red means warmer than the long-term seasonal normal at that grid cell; blue means cooler.</p><p class="demo-muted">This broad context is deliberately wider than the gauge network. It can show where warm water was established, but it cannot independently identify a Kelvin wave at a particular California station.</p></div></article>
        <article class="demo-story-step demo-step demo-story-step--detail" data-scene="historic-sst" data-highlight="historic-sst-context"><div class="demo-story-card demo-story-card--detail"><p class="demo-card-number">An important complication</p><p>The 2015–16 coast was also influenced by the Northeast Pacific marine heatwave, often called the Blob. A broad warm field is relevant context, not a clean attribution.</p><p class="demo-muted">That is why the gauge analysis focuses on measured timing and coverage rather than treating temperature alone as evidence of propagation.</p><p class="demo-source-line">Source: <a href="https://www.ncei.noaa.gov/products/optimum-interpolation-sst" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">NOAA Daily OISST</a> · <a href="https://www.fisheries.noaa.gov/news/blob-overshadows-el-nino" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">NOAA Fisheries on the Blob and El Niño</a></p></div></article>
      </section>
      <section class="demo-scene">
        <div class="demo-sticky"><div class="demo-chart-shell demo-chart-shell--calendar"><div class="demo-chart-kicker">What's happening daily?</div><h3>Residual water levels, by day.</h3><svg id="calendar-chart" aria-label="Daily non-tidal sea-level residual calendar"></svg></div></div>
        <article class="demo-story-step demo-step" data-scene="calendar"><div class="demo-story-card"><p class="demo-card-number">Step 02</p><p>Looking at the daily cells in an attempt to make broader shifts up the coast visible.</p><p class="demo-muted">Color is scaled to the loaded observations. If there's not enough data per day, it shows as "missing" and is dark/empty.</p></div></article>
        <article class="demo-story-step demo-step demo-story-step--detail" data-scene="calendar" data-highlight="calendar-peaks"><div class="demo-story-card demo-story-card--detail"><p class="demo-card-number">Step 02 / A closer read</p><p>They're ordered by their distance from San Diego moving north.</p><p>A coordinated sea level change should (maybe) arrive progressively later as it travels north. Similar patterns may suggest it, but we can't assume it.</p><p class="demo-muted">The next chart gets more granular in 3-hour station measurements around a station's peak water level.</p></div></article>
      </section>
      <section class="demo-scene">
        <div class="demo-sticky"><div class="demo-chart-shell demo-chart-shell--focus"><div class="demo-chart-kicker">Zoom in: Looking closer at coastal candidates</div><h3>Do the dates line up? Will this show something useful?</h3><svg id="focus-chart" aria-label="Station strips and timing marks for a candidate residual water level peaks"></svg></div></div>
        <article class="demo-story-step demo-step" data-scene="focus"><div class="demo-story-card"><p class="demo-card-number">Step 03</p><p class="demo-focus-date">Reading the latest observed days.</p><p>Each strip is a station's own measurements. The lines identifies a potential candidate peak from the southern reference gauge. The circles highlight the neighboring measured bins with similar heights.</p><p class="demo-muted">Still not a clear sign of a Kelvin wave.</p></div></article>
        <article class="demo-story-step demo-step demo-story-step--detail" data-scene="focus" data-highlight="focus-alignment"><div class="demo-story-card demo-story-card--detail"><p class="demo-card-number">Step 03 / The question</p><p>Do similar changes show up farther north later, in a way that suggests they could be related?</p><p class="demo-muted">The full dashboard keeps the coverage and lag tests alongside this visual check.</p></div></article>
      </section>
      <section class="demo-scene">
        <div class="demo-sticky"><div class="demo-chart-shell demo-chart-shell--satellite"><div class="demo-chart-kicker">Zoom out: Off-shore satellite measures</div><h3>What changed offshore?</h3><svg id="satellite-focus-chart" aria-label="Short-term satellite sea-level anomaly change at offshore California points"></svg></div></div>
        <article class="demo-story-step demo-step" data-scene="satellite"><div class="demo-story-card"><p class="demo-card-number">04</p><p>Copernicus satellite data adds a broader offshore reading. It's mentioned as a more common way to show/find our coastal Kelvin. Each block shows how much higher or lower the satellite-estimated sea level is compared with the average for the area over the previous seven days.</p><p class="demo-muted">These pull in large 0.125° gridded samples (12-14 km area) and sit further offshore. They're more likely to show changes in sea level of the slower, warm Kelvin. It provides context for better understanding the above coastal gauges (but not a substitute).</p></div></article>
        <article class="demo-story-step demo-step demo-story-step--detail" data-scene="satellite"><div class="demo-story-card demo-story-card--detail"><p class="demo-card-number">04 / An overhead view</p><p>Similar to above, the satellite rows are offshore grid-cell estimates, not the tide-gauge measurements. I included them to show whether broader sea-level change is visible beyond the coastline.</p></div></article>
      </section>
      <section class="demo-events" aria-labelledby="events-title"><div class="demo-events__intro"><p class="demo-events__eyebrow">Season field log</p><h3 id="events-title">Events to watch, without overclaiming.</h3><p>Reported impacts, agency notices, and data-analysis notes collected as the season unfolds. An event can establish timing or impact; it does not establish a Kelvin-wave cause on its own.</p></div><div class="demo-events__list" id="season-events" aria-live="polite"><p class="demo-events__loading">Loading season events…</p></div></section>
      <section class="kelvin-conclusion"><video class="kelvin-conclusion__video" autoplay muted loop playsinline preload="metadata" aria-hidden="true"><source src="/static/kelvinwaves/assets/conclusion-beach.mp4" type="video/mp4" /></video><div class="kelvin-conclusion__wash" aria-hidden="true"></div><div class="kelvin-conclusion__grain" aria-hidden="true"></div><div class="kelvin-conclusion__content"><p class="kelvin-conclusion__eyebrow">Conclusion (so far)</p><h4 class="kelvin-conclusion__heading">Still looking for a clear signal. What would you try next? Any tips on how to improve detection – Sea Surface Temperature? Other stations? Other data?</h4></div><a class="kelvin-conclusion__link" href="/static/kelvinwaves/index.html">Open the full coastal dashboard →</a><p class="kelvin-conclusion__sources">Gauge observations and astronomical tide predictions: <a href="https://tidesandcurrents.noaa.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">NOAA CO-OPS</a> · Offshore sea-level context: <a href="https://marine.copernicus.eu/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Copernicus Marine Service / DUACS</a></p></section>
    </main>
    <div class="demo-loading" role="status"><span></span> Loading NOAA tide-gauge observations</div>
    </div>
    <script is:inline src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/d3@7"></script><script is:inline src="https://unpkg.com/scrollama"></script><script is:inline src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/gsap@3/dist/gsap.min.js"></script><script is:inline src="/static/kelvinwaves/demo/data.js"></script><script is:inline src="/static/kelvinwaves/demo/charts.js"></script><script is:inline src="/static/kelvinwaves/demo/scroll.js"></script><script is:inline src="/static/kelvinwaves/demo/events.js"></script><script is:inline src="/static/kelvinwaves/demo/demo.js"></script>
  </body>
</html>
